index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..7079ad01b41183e398c4ce37d4436c7d15c0bfbf
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,198 @@
+# arXiv:1802.10566 — verified SLSN blueprint generator (parked)
+
+**Status: `cap_bound`, not shipped and not rejected.** The local generator is
+correct, but the no-tool pool solved every tested level through the largest
+allowed compact route. The next useful increase would cross G9's 300-operation
+cap, so the paper returns to the pool rather than being mislabeled as easy or
+hard.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | B — an efficient algorithm is disclosed |
+| native domain | combinatorics |
+| object regime | finite discrete |
+| computational core | CSP/SAT compatibility search |
+| certificate | integer tuple encoding an SLSN subgraph blueprint |
+| intuition | invariant: one additive gauge is consistent around every color-group triangle |
+| domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| reduction | paper-central, Section 4.2.3, Lemmas 4.10–4.11 and Theorem 4.4 |
+
+## What the problem is and why the checker is trustworthy
+
+[Babay, Dinitz, and Zhang, *Characterizing Demand Graphs for the
+(Fixed-Parameter) Shallow-Light Steiner Network Problem*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.10566)
+asks for a minimum-cost subgraph in which every demand pair is joined within a
+common length bound (Definition 1.1). This generator uses the paper's two-root
+`H_{2,k}` construction. It gives the solver a compact exact description of the
+weighted SLSN graph, all `2k(k-1)` demands, and allowed cross-color offsets. The
+answer chooses one paper vertex gadget per color; the checker expands the proof's
+canonical subgraph, sums integer costs, and runs exact Dijkstra searches from
+both roots to every terminal. It never reads `inst["answer"]`.
+
+Generation is inverse and theorem-backed. Uniform random gauges are sampled
+first; their consistent pair offsets are planted, while every pair receives the
+same number of uniform decoy offsets. Lemma 4.10 maps the held multicolored clique
+to a cost-`g(H)` SLSN subgraph, and Lemma 4.11 supplies the converse structure.
+The displayed weight-4 edges are precisely the paper's internally unique
+four-hop unit paths compressed into one edge, preserving cost and distance.
+
+## Track B difficulty and the paper's easy regimes
+
+This is not a Track A distributional-hardness claim. Generic path consistency
+over every compact difference relation solves all generated instances in
+`O(k^3 d^2)` modular support tests. On eight nominal hard instances
+(`k=58,m=389,d=2`) it solved 8/8, averaging 141,608 tests and 0.049 seconds. The
+compact route fixes the global translation at group 0 and tests the two possible
+anchor offsets against the remaining groups, at most 282 modular membership
+operations. The intended difficulty was recognizing that additive gauge without
+a tool; the oracle results show that the tested models did recognize it.
+
+The demand count grows and the demand graph is neither a star nor constant-size,
+avoiding Theorem 2.2's `n^{O(p^4)}` constant-demand algorithm and Theorem 2.3's
+FPT star-demand algorithm. Theorem 2.4 proves W[1]-hardness for the remaining
+demand classes, but that worst-case theorem is not presented as evidence for
+this structured distribution.
+
+## Worked demo (`seed=0`)
+
+This is the complete rendered instance; a person can solve it on paper by
+checking the two offsets in each of six rows.
+
+```text
+Shallow-Light Steiner Network subgraph blueprint
+
+All indices and residues below are zero-based.  Arithmetic in the D table is
+modulo m=11.  There are k=4 color groups.  Group i has candidate
+vertices V(i,a), one for every residue 0 <= a < m.  Its global vertex ID is
+i*m+a.  For i<j, V(i,a) and V(j,b) are compatible exactly when
+(b-a) mod m belongs to D[i,j].  The complete table is:
+
+  D[0,2] = {10, 8}
+  D[1,3] = {1, 9}
+  D[1,2] = {10, 0}
+  D[0,1] = {4, 10}
+  D[0,3] = {0, 1}
+  D[2,3] = {5, 1}
+
+This table compactly defines the following UNDIRECTED weighted graph from
+Section 4.2 of the paper.  Every displayed edge has both length and cost equal
+to its weight; an edge of weight w is shorthand for an internally unique
+w-hop unit-length/unit-cost path.
+
+Vertices:
+  roots R1,R2;
+  Z(i,j) for i<j;
+  ZE(i,a,j,b) for every compatible i<j pair;
+  Y(i) and YV(i,a);
+  X(i,a,j) and terminal L(i,j) for every ordered i!=j.
+
+Edges (and no others):
+  R1--Z(i,j) [1];
+  Z(i,j)--ZE(i,a,j,b) [1] for compatible pairs;
+  ZE(i,a,j,b)--X(i,a,j) [1] and --X(j,b,i) [1];
+  R2--Y(i) [1], Y(i)--YV(i,a) [1];
+  YV(i,a)--X(i,a,j) [1] for i!=j;
+  X(i,a,j)--L(i,j) [4].
+
+There are 24 demands: for every ordered pair i!=j, both
+{R1,L(i,j)} and {R2,L(i,j)} must be connected in the chosen subgraph by a
+path of length at most 7.  The subgraph's total edge cost must be exactly 92.
+
+Your certificate is a compact subgraph blueprint.  Choose exactly one V(i,a_i)
+from every group, in increasing group order.  Because adding one residue to all
+a_i changes no compatibility, normalize this symmetry by choosing V(0,0), whose
+global ID is 0.  The checker expands the canonical paper subgraph: for each i<j
+it uses ZE(i,a_i,j,a_j) and its four incident proof edges, and for each ordered
+i!=j it uses the R2/Y/YV chain and the edge to L(i,j).  Thus a certificate is
+valid only if every chosen pair is compatible, the expanded union has exact cost
+92, and all demands meet the closed (inclusive) length bound.  Different valid
+blueprints are accepted.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as 4 comma-separated
+global vertex IDs in increasing group order, with no brackets.
+Example format: <answer>0, 11, 22, 33</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+Answer: `<answer>0, 21, 32, 33</answer>`.
+`verify(inst, [0,21,32,33]) == (True, "ok")`; changing the last ID gives
+`verify(inst, [0,21,32,34]) == (False, "chosen candidates are not pairwise compatible")`.
+
+## Presets and gates
+
+| preset | k | modulus | offsets/pair | demands | outcome |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 4 | 11 | 2 | 24 | hand-scale example |
+| easy | 24 | 97 | 2 | 1,104 | oracle solved 3/3 |
+| medium | 40 | 193 | 2 | 3,120 | oracle solved 2/3; one invalid tuple |
+| hard (nominal) | 58 | 389 | 2 | 6,612 | oracle solved 3/3; not shipped |
+| escalated | 60 | 491 | 2 | 7,080 | oracle solved 3/3; next rung is `cap_bound` |
+
+| gate | measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | planted verified 12/12 across all presets |
+| G2 | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | prose/fence/tag round-trip passed; answer JSON-native |
+| G4 | 0/200,000 normalized structure-aware guesses; language size about `2^491` |
+| G5 | exactly one normalized witness by construction; strongest failing restart tried 2,048 candidates in 1.152 s |
+| G6 | five attacks, 0/8 successes each; reference path consistency 8/8 |
+| G7 | doubled `n=116` instance verified; search space grew from 491 to 1,107 bits |
+| G8 | 100/100 affine/class relabellings invariant and carried answers valid; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | 319 chars, 117 tokens, 58 atoms, 282 intended operations |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostic
+
+| preset | seed | model | result | verifier reason |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| easy | 1928683793 | GPT-5.6 Terra | solved | ok |
+| easy | 682470459 | Gemini 3.8 Flash | solved | ok |
+| easy | 851653858 | Gemini 3.8 Flash | solved | ok |
+| medium | 448864506 | GPT-5.6 Terra | failed | chosen candidates not pairwise compatible |
+| medium | 343425352 | Gemini 3.8 Flash | solved | ok |
+| medium | 4128182 | GPT-5.6 Terra | solved | ok |
+| hard | 120421877 | Gemini 3.8 Flash | solved | ok |
+| hard | 405866626 | GPT-5.6 Terra | solved | ok |
+| hard | 2146182354 | GPT-5.6 Terra | solved | ok |
+| escalated | 277475886 | Gemini 3.8 Flash | solved | ok |
+| escalated | 1212827504 | GPT-5.6 Terra | solved | ok |
+| escalated | 2024336430 | GPT-5.6 Terra | solved | ok |
+
+| arm | solved/attempts | conclusion |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare, nominal hard | 3/3 | the invariant is discoverable without a hint |
+| structural hint | not run | terminal `cap_bound` requires stopping |
+| placebo hint | not run | terminal `cap_bound` requires stopping |
+
+`hinted - placebo` is therefore not estimable. No `g9_*_transcript.jsonl` files
+were fabricated; the instruction to stop on `cap_bound` takes precedence. The
+script-owned bare transcript and `.meta.json` contain the terminal verdict.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_1802_10566 as g
+
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY["demo"])
+text = g.render(inst)
+answer = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + ", ".join(map(str, inst["answer"])) + "</answer>")
+assert g.verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, the mechanical emitter command is
+`bash scripts/emit.sh 1802.10566 20 hard`. Do not add this parked family to a
+release unless a future harness raises the intended-route cap and a fresh
+`harden.py` run returns `hardened`.
+
+## Caveats
+
+The compact offset encoding exposes more of the source reduction than an
+explicit million-edge SLSN graph would, and the oracle results show this makes
+the gauge recoverable. The zero-hit estimate samples uniformly after enforcing
+shape, group ranges, and the stated group-0 normalization; it does not model an
+informed solver's highly nonuniform prior. The path-consistency reference is
+stronger than the tested heuristics on this distribution, but no ILP/SAT package
+was run. The canonical key is invariant under class permutations, independent
+candidate translations, common affine multipliers, and input reordering; it is
+a strong cheap cycle signature, not a complete SLSN graph-isomorphism canonizer.
+Most importantly, Theorem 2.4 is worst-case W[1]-hardness and says nothing about
+the generated synchronized distribution.
